WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.720
Hey look, Mom, I'm studying Yiddish.
So I can talk to Grandma.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.260
[Klezmer music]

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.740
Good day, guys. This is just a quick video
 to answer a question that I got.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:20.200
And I decided to make it into a video
 because I've had this question before

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.380
and maybe, it's something that 
a lot of people are wondering.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.120
The question is : 
"Are Hebrew and Yiddish similar?"

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.840
Or "are Hebrew and Yiddish kind of the same language?"

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.320
The answer to that is "No". 
They are different languages.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.360
They are often confused with each other because
the Yiddish language is written in the Hebrew alphabet,

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.760
in the same way that Farsi or Urdu 
are written in the Arabic alphabet,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.820
or that German is written 
in the Roman alphabet just like English is.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.980
So someone who doesn't read that script might look
at it  and think that it's the same language.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.640
But Hebrew and Yiddish are different.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.980
Another reason they might be confused is 
that they are both related to Jewish people.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.020
But they're related to Jewish people 
in different ways, so...

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.480
Hebrew is the old language of the Bible,
of the Old Testament,

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.480
of the Hebrew Bible - as they would call it -, the Torah.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.420
And it's also been a literary language 
of Jews throughout the centuries.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.820
Often, for religious purposes or literary reasons, 
they would write in Hebrew.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.000
But it was just a literary language for a long time.
It died as a spoken language.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.540
But Yiddish was spoken by Jews in Europe.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:36.960
And it's... The language it's mostly related to is German.
It's kind of a medieval form of German.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.040
But I understand that, to some extent, Yiddish speakers
can communicate with German speakers.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.420
And they can understand each other.
Not fully, but to some extent.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:51.540
So it's mostly related to German, but it's been influenced 
by Hebrew in terms of vocabulary.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.980
And also by Aramaic 
and maybe some other languages too.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.720
Because Hebrew and Aramaic were
 Jewish literary languages.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.120
So there is some Hebrew vocabulary in it,
but I think the pronunciation has kind of
been changed and more Germanized.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.380
So it doesn't sound like Hebrew so much.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.020
So Yiddish was influenced by Hebrew 
to some extent though.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.280
And also Modern Hebrew 
was influenced by Yiddish because...

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.880
the original Zionists who came to Palestine,
to create a Jewish state,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.240
they were Yiddish speakers, I think, Yiddish,
 maybe Russian or whatever.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:30.600
But their native language, Yiddish, maybe also Russian 
and others influenced Modern Hebrew.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.360
So people say that Modern Hebrew syntax and phonology 
reflects the Yiddish language to some extent.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:42.080
So Yiddish has influenced Modern Hebrew as well, 
but basically, they're separate languages.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.500
And you can't communicate with someone 
who speaks the other.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:51.420
It would be easier for a Yiddish speaker to communicate 
with someone who speaks German, I think.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:57.200
Alright, so... Hopefully that is helpful. 
And just a quick whole answer to that question.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.740
Aright, talk to you later...

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:06.520
[Klezmer music]

